            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row that explains everything else is scoop size. FlavCity's scoop weighs 49.5 g, almost twice the 27 g Clean Simple Eats scoop, because roughly half of it is not protein: cocoa, coconut milk powder, banana powder, sea salt and the two mushroom powders. That is why FlavCity carries twice the calories, six times the carbohydrate and eight times the fat while giving only 5 g more protein. For a broader view of how these two sit against the rest of the market, our <a href="/protein-powder-comparison/">protein powder comparison</a> table ranks every label we have checked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-smoothie" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is a Protein Smoothie, and Is FlavCity Really a Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity's Protein Smoothie is a whey-based powder with food ingredients added, so it sits somewhere between a protein powder and a meal-replacement shake. The ingredient list on the chocolate bag starts with grass-fed undenatured whey protein concentrate, then grass-fed collagen, organic cocoa, coconut milk powder, grass-fed milk protein concentrate, fermented nutritional yeast protein and banana powder, followed by sea salt, two forms of stevia, and organic cordyceps and reishi powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            The word smoothie is doing real work here. A plain protein powder like Clean Simple Eats is meant to be mostly protein, so you add your own milk, fruit or oats. FlavCity has built some of those extras into the bag: the banana and coconut milk add body, natural sugar and fat, which is why one scoop shaken with water lands at 200 calories with 13 g of carbohydrate and 3 g of fiber. That makes it more filling than a lean whey shake, and it is closer to a light breakfast on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the collagen. FlavCity says each scoop holds 10 g of grass-fed collagen, and its panel lists 25 g of total protein, so the collagen appears to be counted inside the 25 g rather than on top of it. On that reading, a scoop carries about 15 g of complete protein from whey, milk and yeast, plus about 10 g of collagen. Collagen is an incomplete protein: it lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, the amino acid most tied to muscle building, so most sports nutritionists would not count it toward a post-workout protein target.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a small labeling wrinkle. FlavCity's own Amazon store, run through the seller Pattern, titled its listings 20 g protein with 10 g collagen when we checked on 19 November 2026, while the brand's current nutrition panel shows 25 g. We use the panel figure throughout this page, but if you buy on Amazon, read the image of the bag you are actually sent. Our <a href="/flavcity-protein-powder-review/">FlavCity protein powder review</a> covers the rest of the range, including the PB&amp;J and plant-based versions.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT IT MEANS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Count FlavCity as about 15 g of complete protein per scoop</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are using a shake to hit a muscle-building protein target, treat each FlavCity scoop as roughly 15 g of whey, milk and yeast protein plus 10 g of collagen. Clean Simple Eats gives 20 g of whey isolate per scoop, so it delivers more complete protein in half the calories.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-per-scoop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, FlavCity or Clean Simple Eats?
          </h2>

          <p>
            FlavCity has more total protein per scoop, 25 g against 20 g, but Clean Simple Eats has more complete protein per scoop, about 20 g against roughly 15 g once collagen is set aside. Which number matters depends on why you are drinking the shake. For general daily protein intake, total grams count. For recovery after training, complete protein with a full amino acid profile is what the research on muscle protein synthesis measures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Density tells the same story from another angle. Clean Simple Eats is about 74 percent protein by weight, which is normal for a flavored whey isolate. FlavCity is about 51 percent protein by weight, lower than almost any dedicated protein powder, because half the scoop is food ingredients. Per 100 calories, Clean Simple Eats gives 20 g of protein and FlavCity gives 12.5 g, so if you track calories, Clean Simple Eats is the far more efficient source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats does have its own weakness here: 20 g is on the low side for a whey isolate, and many isolates hit 25 g per scoop. To reach 25 g you need about 1.25 scoops, which is roughly 34 g of powder and 125 calories. That is still well under one 200-calorie FlavCity scoop, and every gram of it is whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats is cheaper per gram of protein at brand-site prices: about $2.71 per 25 g against $3.00 for FlavCity. We calculate cost per 25 g as the bag price divided by total grams of protein in the bag, multiplied by 25. A $64.99 Clean Simple Eats bag holds 30 scoops of 20 g, or 600 g of protein; a $59.99 FlavCity bag holds 20 scoops of 25 g, or 500 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats is also cheaper per scoop, about $2.17 against $3.00, which surprises people who compare sticker prices. The FlavCity bag costs $5 less, but it holds 10 fewer servings. If you think of each scoop as one shake, Clean Simple Eats saves you roughly 83 cents a shake at these prices, or about $25 a month for someone drinking one a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap widens if you only count complete protein. Treat FlavCity's 10 g of collagen as excluded and each $3.00 scoop buys about 15 g of whey, milk and yeast protein, which works out to about $5.00 per 25 g of complete protein. Clean Simple Eats stays at $2.71 because all of its protein is whey isolate.
          </p>

