        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is FlavCity or Clean Simple Eats Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats says its protein is third-party tested; FlavCity makes no such claim; and neither names an independent certifier such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice. That gives Clean Simple Eats a narrow edge, but it is a brand statement rather than a result you can look up, and we found no published certificates of analysis for either product.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity's bag carries a California Proposition 65 warning for lead and cadmium. A Prop 65 warning is not a test result: California's thresholds are very low, and products can carry the notice without exceeding any federal limit. It does mean the brand considers at least some ingredients, likely the cocoa, plant and mushroom powders, worth flagging. Clean Simple Eats Brownie Batter also contains cocoa, and chocolate flavors in general tend to carry more of these metals than vanilla.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity also notes on the bag that it is made on shared equipment that may contain peanuts or tree nuts, which matters for anyone with those allergies. Both products contain milk. Clean Simple Eats lists coconut milk powder in its ingredients but declares only milk as an allergen, so readers who avoid coconut should note it is in both.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a drug-tested athlete, the honest answer is that neither fits: you need a product listed in a certification database. For everyone else, our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains how to weigh a brand claim against a named certification, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">heavy-metal guide</a> covers brands that publish batch results.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Has the Cleaner Label, FlavCity or Clean Simple Eats?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats has the shorter and cleaner label by most definitions: six ingredients plus a 100 mg enzyme blend, against twelve in FlavCity Chocolate. Its list reads grass-fed whey protein isolate, cocoa powder, natural flavors, sea salt, coconut milk powder and stevia leaf extract. The brand also markets the current formula as free of xylitol and xanthan gum, which earlier versions used.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity's case for a clean label is different. It uses no natural or artificial flavors in the chocolate bag, sweetens with stevia only, and claims 0 g of added sugar and no GMOs (a brand claim with no certifier named). Every ingredient is a recognizable food or protein, which is the point of a brand that grew out of Bobby Parrish's label-reading cooking channel. The trade-off is length and dilution: more ingredients means less protein per gram and more for a reader to evaluate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The functional extras deserve skepticism. FlavCity does not list how much cordyceps or reishi is in a scoop, and the human evidence for either at small doses is thin, so we would not pay extra for them. Collagen has some evidence for skin and joint comfort, but at the cost of displacing complete protein in the 25 g total. Clean Simple Eats keeps it simple, with the enzyme blend as its only add-on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our Label Score reflects all of this: 48 out of 100 for Clean Simple Eats and 23 for FlavCity. The score rewards protein density, complete protein, sweetener choice and verified testing, and penalizes low density and unverified claims. Neither scores highly because neither has a named certification. For the cleanest-scoring options across the market, see our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> ranking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers generally rate both brands well for taste, and we have not tasted either, so this section relies on retailer reviews and the labels. Taste is the main reason people buy Clean Simple Eats; buyer feedback on its flavors has been mostly positive, though the recent reformulation has divided some long-time customers, a few of whom report a different taste or aftertaste in the new formula.
          </p>

          <p>
            FlavCity's label points to a thicker, creamier drink. Coconut milk powder, banana powder and 4 g of fat per scoop give a fuller body than a whey isolate shaken in water, which is what most people want from something called a smoothie. The brand suggests mixing a serving with about 12 oz of milk or water. Clean Simple Eats, at 0.5 g of fat, will be thinner in water and works best in milk, oats or baking if you want richness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor range is close. Clean Simple Eats lists flavors such as Brownie Batter, Simply Vanilla, Snickerdoodle, Cookies 'N Cream, Cake Batter, Toasted Marshmallow and an Unflavored version, which is useful for cooking. FlavCity offers Chocolate, Vanilla Cream, two PB&amp;J flavors, Pumpkin Spice Latte, Banana Bread, Butter Coffee, Cinnamon Roll and more. Whichever you choose, a smaller bag or single-serve packet is the low-risk way to test a flavor first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-goal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Clean Simple Eats vs FlavCity for Weight Loss, Smoothies and Baking
          </h2>

          <p>
            For weight loss, Clean Simple Eats is the stronger tool: 20 g of protein in 100 calories gives far more satiety per calorie than FlavCity's 200-calorie scoop. FlavCity can still suit a calorie-controlled plan if it replaces a whole breakfast rather than being added to one, since its fiber and fat make it more filling than a lean shake. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">protein powder for weight loss</a> guide covers the trade-off in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            For smoothies, FlavCity is the more convenient pick because the banana and coconut milk are already in the bag; shake it with milk and you have breakfast. Clean Simple Eats makes a better smoothie base if you add your own fruit, since it brings protein without many extra calories. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">protein powder for smoothies</a> guide shows how to build one around a plain whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            For baking, Clean Simple Eats is clearly better. An Unflavored option, 100 calories a scoop and a lean label make it easy to fold into pancakes, muffins and protein oats. FlavCity's banana, coconut and mushroom powders bring flavors that may not suit every recipe, and you pay more per gram for them. For muscle gain, Clean Simple Eats again wins, because more of each scoop is complete whey protein.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What FlavCity and Clean Simple Eats Do Well, and Where They Fall Short
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">FlavCity Protein Smoothie</h3>

          <p>
            FlavCity does the ready-made smoothie well. It uses grass-fed whey concentrate and milk protein, sweetens only with stevia, claims 0 g of added sugar, and adds real banana and coconut milk for body. It is widely sold, through its own store, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop per the brand's list of authorized retailers, though not Costco. Where it falls short is protein value: about half the scoop is not protein, about 10 g of the 25 g is collagen, there is no testing claim, and the bag carries a Prop 65 warning for lead and cadmium.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Simple Eats Whey Protein</h3>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats does lean, flavorful whey well: 20 g of grass-fed isolate in 100 calories, a six-ingredient label, stevia, no xylitol and a big dessert-flavor range. It is cheaper per scoop and per gram than FlavCity in the 30-serving bag. Its weaknesses are a 20 g scoop that trails most isolates, a high price against mainstream whey, smaller bags that cost much more per gram, and a testing claim with no named certifier. Our <a href="/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/">Clean Simple Eats protein powder review</a> covers the clear protein and stick packs too.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are also weighing Clean Simple Eats against another clean-label whey that adds collagen, our sibling head-to-head <a href="/clean-simple-eats-vs-just-ingredients/">Clean Simple Eats vs Just Ingredients</a> runs the same label-by-label check, and <a href="/orgain-vs-truvani/">Orgain vs Truvani</a> compares two more real-food brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Which Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Lifters and anyone chasing a protein target:</strong> buy Clean Simple Eats. All of its protein is whey isolate, it costs $2.71 per 25 g in the big bag, and 1.25 scoops gets you 25 g for about 125 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Busy mornings with no blender:</strong> buy FlavCity. One scoop with milk is close to a light breakfast, with fiber, fat and fruit already in the bag and you pay $3.00 a scoop at brand-site prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Calorie counters and bakers:</strong> buy Clean Simple Eats. Half the calories per scoop, an Unflavored option and a short label make it the better ingredient.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Collagen fans:</strong> FlavCity is the only one of the two with collagen built in. Just remember you are trading complete protein for it, and a separate collagen supplement plus a whey isolate is often cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Amazon shoppers:</strong> FlavCity's official Amazon store had the best per-scoop price we saw at $50.99 for 20 servings, but check that the bag's panel matches the listing before you rely on the 25 g figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drug-tested athletes should skip both, because neither has an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice listing; pick a certified isolate from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide instead. Budget buyers should skip both too: at $2.55 to $3.75 per 25 g across the purchases above, either one costs well over mainstream whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with a milk allergy should skip both, since each is dairy-based, and anyone who is lactose sensitive should be more cautious with FlavCity, whose whey and milk protein concentrates carry more lactose than an isolate. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers alternatives. If you avoid stevia, neither works, because both are sweetened with it. And anyone pregnant, buying for a child, or managing kidney disease should ask a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein product.
          </p>
        </div>

