            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Clean Simple Eats vs FlavCity: which has more protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  FlavCity lists more total protein, 25 g per scoop against 20 g for Clean Simple Eats, per their chocolate labels. But FlavCity says about 10 g of its scoop is collagen, an incomplete protein, so it carries roughly 15 g of complete protein. Clean Simple Eats has about 20 g of complete whey isolate protein, so it wins on the protein that builds muscle.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is FlavCity protein a protein powder or a meal replacement?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  FlavCity sits in between. It is a whey-based powder with banana, coconut milk, cocoa and mushroom powders added, so a scoop has 200 calories, 13 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of fiber and 4 g of fat. That is more than a plain protein powder but less than a full meal-replacement shake. Mixed with milk, it works as a light breakfast.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are FlavCity and Clean Simple Eats third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither has a named certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice. Clean Simple Eats says its protein is third-party tested but does not name the lab or publish results. FlavCity makes no testing claim and its bag carries a California Prop 65 warning for lead and cadmium, which is a notice, not a measured result.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is cheaper, FlavCity or Clean Simple Eats?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Clean Simple Eats is cheaper in its 30-serving bag: about $2.17 per scoop and $2.71 per 25 g of protein at $64.99. FlavCity costs $3.00 per scoop and per 25 g at $59.99 for 20 servings on its site. FlavCity's Amazon store price of $50.99 brings it to about $2.55, and Clean Simple Eats' smaller bags cost more per gram.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for weight loss, FlavCity or Clean Simple Eats?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Clean Simple Eats is usually the better choice for weight loss because it gives 20 g of protein in only 100 calories, against FlavCity's 25 g in 200 calories. Protein per calorie drives satiety on a diet. FlavCity can still work if it replaces a full breakfast, since its fiber and fat make it more filling than a lean shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do FlavCity and Clean Simple Eats use artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Both are sweetened with stevia only, per their labels at the time of writing: FlavCity uses organic stevia Reb A and stevia Reb M, and Clean Simple Eats uses stevia leaf extract. Neither lists sucralose, acesulfame potassium or sugar alcohols, and Clean Simple Eats says its current formula is xylitol-free. If stevia's aftertaste bothers you, neither one avoids it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

